WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.870
LARRY P. ARNN: Bob Woodson
is both brave and sensible.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.880
He's been a leader in
the civil rights movement

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.610
since he was hardly more than
a boy, in the early 1960s.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.530
That was not always a safe
thing to do back then.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.822
He is tough and
principled and kind.

00:00:17.822 --> 00:00:20.400
He has a master's
degree in social work

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.280
from the University of
Pennsylvania, an Ivy League

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.750
institution.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.810
In the '60s, he joined
the civil rights movement.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:33.130
From '71 to '73, he
headed a major project

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.680
at the National Urban
League on Criminal Justice

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.300
in New York City.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.550
In 1974, he became
a resident fellow

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.590
at the American Enterprise
Institute, in Washington.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.690
By this time he'd been
doing a lot of thinking.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:50.260
And he began to build a
movement, of which he's now

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.050
the godfather.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.200
He's the inventor of
the most promising thing

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.130
in race relations
in America today,

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.160
but more than race relations--

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.830
in assistance to
people who've got tough

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:06.520
lives and haven't had a chance.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.490
He calls it the neighborhood
empowerment movement.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:17.410
And he brought this work
a common-sense observation

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.340
that's too much forgotten.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.050
Troubled areas can be improved
by the same principles that

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.050
work everywhere.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.610
And they can really
only be improved

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.200
by the people who live in them.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.680
So he began to look for
people who are like that.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.470
We've just been reminiscing.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.390
It's a miracle we're actually
getting around to this lecture,

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.910
because he and I could
sit and talk all day.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.910
But we were remembering
a particular one of them

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:49.340
that I knew very well.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.960
There was a man
named Leon Watkins,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.900
and he lived in Watts, which
is the place where the riots

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:59.330
started in '68 and later in LA.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.940
And he made the newspapers--
and I read the article--

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:08.570
because he put up wanted posters
all over Watts with a picture

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.910
of the head of the Crips--

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:14.510
a gang-- the Bloods were the
other big gang back then--

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.880
and his name on it.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.230
Wanted.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.900
He put his address on there.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.750
And so the day came when
the head of the Crips

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:28.140
drove up to his place with an
entourage, carrying weapons.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.630
And he said, what
do you want with me?

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:38.195
And Leon says, it's not I
who wants you, it's Jesus.

00:02:38.195 --> 00:02:40.590
[LAUGHS] I heard that story.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.630
I just love that story.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.203
And so I went down and met him--

00:02:44.203 --> 00:02:44.870
got to know him.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.990
But I went down and met
him through Bob Woodson.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:49.400
And he has helped build--

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.840
Bob's just telling me--

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.050
Bob Woodson's enterprise that
reaches all over the country

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.160
now.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.880
And it focuses on
people who are heroes,

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.190
and who are trying to
build a decent life

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:04.220
on the old principles
of what decency is.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.370
And those principles are not
black and they're not white.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:08.390
They are what they are.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.370
They're human.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.080
And I want you to
know, I've always

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.640
found him an inspiring man.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.960
There's a fire
that burns in Bob.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.900
It warms everything he
comes in contact with,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.300
for all the time I've known him.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.700
But it doesn't burn him
up, because the fire

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.230
is sparked by love.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.660
And he maintains
that all the time.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:33.410
Robert Woodson.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.865
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:44.227 --> 00:03:45.310
ROBERT WOODSON: Thank you.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.400
I have to say that this is
the first time in my life

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.390
that I've ever been
introduced by someone

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:58.725
who nearly brought me to tears.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.680
Larry reminded me of how
we met and when we met

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:11.060
and the precious
person of Leon Watkins.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.050
There is a prayer that I
utter each time that I speak,

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:16.617
and I commend to you.

00:04:16.617 --> 00:04:18.200
And that is, God,
give me the strength

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.480
to tell and pursue the
truth, especially when

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:21.680
it's inconvenient to me.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.580
That if you want to go
someplace that you haven't been,

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:33.330
you have to be willing to do
something you haven't done.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.630
Or to put it another way, if
you keep doing what you do,

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.600
you keep getting what
you got, to quote

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.950
one of my grassroots leaders.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:48.320
George Bernard Shaw asked
a rhetorical question,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.710
"Must a Christ die in
every age to save those

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:55.000
that lack imagination?"

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.340
And Einstein said
that, "Imagination is

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:01.460
more important than knowledge."

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.880
Ladies and gentlemen,
at no other time

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:12.560
do we need moral imagination
in the defense of this country.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.240
As Larry said, I came as a
veteran of the civil rights

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:15.740
movement.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.470
But I must tell you a
little bit about my journey.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:25.870
I don't define myself
as a conservative.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:32.580
I define my political philosophy
as radical pragmatism.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.640
And I am a cardiac
Christian, because Christ

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.410
burns in my heart.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:42.250
And so therefore that defines.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:46.750
And so what I do, then,
is look for allies

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.950
that fits into my
mission-- and that

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.320
is to serve the least
of God's children.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:59.210
My life is dedicated to serving
the least of God's children.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.890
The quality of any
person's life, or a nation

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:05.260
is determined by,
how does it affect

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.280
the least of God's children?

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.330
So when I'm looking
for political allies,

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.660
I look for people whose
strategic interests

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:16.420
are compatible with
the strategic interests

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:20.240
of the least of God's children.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:25.130
And as a civil rights leader
at the time serving in West

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:27.890
Chester, Pennsylvania--
the home of Bayard Rustin,

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.980
who was a principal
advisor to King--

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:34.910
and they would come through
our town to bring the person.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:38.350
And we would push
our local issues.

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:41.380
And I became disenchanted with
the civil rights movement,

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:45.640
as a young worker,
over two issues.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:50.730
The first one was forced
busing for integration.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:58.000
I believed that the
opposite of segregation

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.020
was desegregation,
that integration

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.690
was an individual matter.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.150
Because I believed
then, as I believe now,

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:13.300
that if you say the
goal is integration,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.940
then you are concluding that
anything that's all Black

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:16.620
is all bad.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.390
And so that got me in
trouble with some people.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.820
Because they said,
well, Bob, your position

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.910
is compatible with the John
Birch Society and the Klan.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:30.880
And my response-- well,
if I like classical music

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.780
and Hitler does, am I supposed
to not like classical music?

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:36.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.500
You should take
your position based

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:42.280
on what's right and compatible
with what you believe,

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:47.170
not who agrees or disagrees
with your position.

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:50.860
The second reason that I left
the civil rights movement is

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:54.880
when we were leading
demonstrations outside of Wyatt

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.560
Laboratory-- a
pharmaceutical company--

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.820
and after two months,
when they desegregated

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.510
and they hired nine
Black PhD chemists,

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:07.840
we asked these brothers and
sisters to join our movement.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.140
They said, we got these jobs
because we were qualified,

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:15.580
not because of the sacrifices
of you all out there picketing.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:20.380
And then I realized that we
have more of a class problem

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:23.840
than we do a race problem.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:29.110
And so I realized that many of
the people who sacrificed most

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:30.970
in the struggle for
civil rights did not

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.429
benefit from the change.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:35.169
Because as Dr. King
said, what good

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:37.150
does it do to have
the right to live

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:41.770
in a neighborhood of your
choosing or eat in a restaurant

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:44.620
if you don't have the
means to exercise or take

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:45.730
advantage of that right?

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:47.950
So it is not just
opening the doors.

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:50.920
We must invest in
people's capacity

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:55.120
to take advantage of what
opportunity is created.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.160
And I believe then
that there was

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:00.850
a big split in the civil
rights movement at that time,

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:02.980
because--

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:07.450
well, the poverty programs
that came along at the time,

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:11.710
instead of being a continued
restoration force, the way

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:13.270
the civil rights
laws were built,

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:16.810
the poverty program
proves to be a virus that

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:20.650
was more injurious to the
welfare of the Black community

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.330
in this nation
than anything that

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:25.510
happened between the end of
slavery up until that point.

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:28.440
And that's a dramatic point.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.990
And I want to unpack
that for a while,

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.120
because I think it is important.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.340
Because this nation
is in crisis right

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:41.740
now, where everything
is being divided.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:44.650
And as I said, my
political philosophy

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:49.460
is radical pragmatism
that I am in favor.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:52.270
And so I ask myself, what
are the policies that

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:54.770
favor the thing?

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:58.810
Now, the left--
the radical left--

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:02.680
has convinced people that the
problems facing Black America

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:04.090
today--

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:08.380
70% out-of-wedlock birth, the
high crime rates, unemployment,

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:13.250
all the other deficiencies--

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:18.230
are as a result of the legacy
of slavery and discrimination

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:20.270
that followed.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:27.530
And so therefore it culminated
in the 1619 essays in 2019

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:31.850
by The New York Times, headed
by Nikole Hannah-Jones, that

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.970
clarified all of that and
said, America's real birth date

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:38.360
is 1619, not 1776.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.880
And therefore America
is incurably racist--

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:44.360
that racism is in its DNA
and that white people should

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:48.620
be shamed, blamed, and
punished, and Black people

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:51.738
should be patronized and paid.

00:10:51.738 --> 00:10:53.570
[LAUGHS]

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:58.150
And therefore because some of
the Founders were slave owners,

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:00.130
and therefore
America's capitalism

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:04.330
is built on a slavetocracy.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:08.380
And so this is what
is pervasive today.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.500
And so what we decided
at the Woodson Center,

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:17.200
because the radical left is
using the legacy of slavery--

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:22.090
I would say, the perversion
of that history--

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:26.860
as the principal bludgeon
to destroy the country--

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:29.650
since they were
using that messenger,

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:33.000
we felt that the messenger
should also look like that.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:39.040
So we brought together
26 scholars and activists

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:46.100
to defend 1776 as the real
birth date of the country.

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:49.930
And so what we were offering--
and not as a counter debate--

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:53.350
we wanted to offer a
counter narrative that

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:56.750
was both inspirational
and aspirational,

00:11:56.750 --> 00:12:02.800
and therefore to teach the
real truth of this country--

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:06.970
that, yes, America,
none of us should

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:11.180
be defined by our birth
defects of slavery.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:13.750
How many of you want to be
defined by the worst things

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:16.570
you've ever done as a child?

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.670
None of you do.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:22.240
Because America is
about second chances.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:27.570
America is a country built on
the foundation of redemption.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:30.020
And so what we
offered, therefore,

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:33.410
was in this counternarrative,
we went back

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:36.020
to unpack that history.

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:41.600
And so in our essays, we
demonstrate that Black America

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.200
should not be defined
by slavery alone,

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:49.280
but we should be defined
by the resilience

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:53.570
and self-determination that
was in evidence in response

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:56.810
to those oppressive conditions--

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:00.740
that most people don't know,
Black or white, the history

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:05.520
of our response to oppression.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.740
One of our essayists
went back and looked

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:11.370
at the records of
six plantations

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:15.540
to see, what was the state of
the Black family in slavery.

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:21.750
He found that 75% of every
one of those slave families

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:24.900
had a man and a woman
raising children.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:30.410
There was a rush to marry
legally right afterwards.

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:35.810
Well, there was a 75% illiteracy
rate among the slaves.

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:39.530
That rate went down to
25% in less than 40 years,

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:45.530
when the Freedmen's Bureau sent
agents south to teach Blacks.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:48.590
They said that the sabbath
schools, or the church schools,

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:52.700
were doing more than anything
the government can do.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.450
Never in the
history of the world

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:59.780
did a people reduce its
illiteracy rate from 75% to 25%

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:02.240
in 40 years--

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:05.990
because of the self-help
efforts undertaken

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.650
by these freed slaves.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:11.510
And it's because they had
these institutions that

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:15.890
stand between the
government and the people,

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.680
that we call
mediating structures.

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:23.900
When Blacks were denied
access to hotels, what we did

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.180
was build our own.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.100
We didn't have
insurance companies,

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.860
so our churches
had burial society.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:35.330
We tapped those funds
to build hotels.

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:37.780
We had the Waluhaje
Hotel in Atlanta,

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:42.730
the Calvert Hotel in Miami,
the St. Theresa in Chicago.

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:47.740
We built that.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:50.910
When we were denied access
to colleges and universities,

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:57.790
we built 100 by the year 2025.

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:01.990
Education gap--
Booker T. Washington

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:07.000
partnered with Sears.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.250
And as a consequence--

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:12.940
Julius Rosenwald was
the CEO of Sears.

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:17.020
He partnered with
Booker T. Washington.

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:20.980
And he was going to manufacturer
schools in his plant.

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:23.410
Booker T. Washington says
that we don't want charity,

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:25.760
we want an investment.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:27.220
So he put up $4 million.

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:31.060
And the Black local
groups raised $4.8 million

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:32.410
in response.

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:36.070
And together they built
5,000 Rosenwald schools

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:39.340
throughout the
South that operated.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:42.580
And as a consequence of
building these schools,

00:15:42.580 --> 00:15:48.730
in 1920, the education gap
between Blacks and whites

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.320
in the South was three years.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.690
It was eight years for
whites, five years for Blacks.

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:58.930
Because of the Rosenwald
school, the education gap,

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:03.120
in less than 20 years,
closed within six months--

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:06.330
when we were crowded
classrooms, used

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:10.170
textbooks, half the
budgets of white schools.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.840
The question is, if we
could close the education

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:16.410
gap in the midst of
segregation, why can't we

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:18.960
close the education
gap today, when

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:23.850
many of those school systems are
being run by people of color?

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.840
The whole issue of marriage--

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:33.320
we know that what helped
Blacks sustain themselves

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:36.500
in the face of slavery
and discrimination

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:40.160
was their two-parent households,
their Christian faith,

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:43.400
and their commitment
to self-determination.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:45.380
Again, let's look
at the records.

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:47.720
Let's look at facts.

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:53.630
Because if we cannot make
decisions based upon fact-based

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:58.470
truths, then lies become normal.

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:05.099
The fact is that in 1930 and
1940, when racism was enshrined

00:17:05.099 --> 00:17:09.060
in the law, there was no
political representation

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:12.000
in government, the
Black community

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:17.640
had the highest marriage rate
of any other group in society.

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:19.980
Elderly people,
as a consequence,

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.319
could walk safely
in their communities

00:17:22.319 --> 00:17:28.300
without fear of being assaulted
by their grandchildren.

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:30.610
The poverty rate in
the Black community,

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:37.180
according to Thomas
Sowell, in 1940 was 85%.

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:38.970
And in 10 years,
it was reduced--

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.010
by 20%.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:56.375
And then by 1960, it
was reduced another 15%.

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:04.800
Up until 1965,
two-parent households

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:08.070
and the incarceration rate
from the turn of the century

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.255
was just 20% to 25%.

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:20.280
And so in 1868, when 1,000
Blacks were fired off

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:22.810
the docks of
Baltimore, Maryland,

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.570
they didn't march on Washington
demanding jobs, peace,

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:26.120
and freedom.

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:28.630
They went to the
burial societies.

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:30.560
And Blacks owned
their own railroad--

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:33.430
the Chesapeake Marine Dry
Dock and Railroad Company,

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:36.730
that hired 1,000 people,
including white workers--

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:42.420
and successfully ran a railroad
from Baltimore to Maine.

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.960
In the city of Chicago that
you hear, in the Brownsville

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:50.210
Section, you talk about
redlining that took place.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:57.560
In 1929, Blacks owned 731
businesses and $100 million

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:05.430
in real-estate assets in what
we called the Black Wall Street.

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:08.260
This was true in
most urban centers.

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:15.200
Remember, just a few
decades away from slavery,

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:16.850
there were 20
Blacks who were born

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:18.800
slaves who died millionaires.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:28.980
What sustained Blacks
was not becoming

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:33.420
bitter about their
circumstance, but working.

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:35.880
There is an example,
for instance,

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.130
about what I call
radical grace, that's

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.180
at the heart of the
Black community.

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:41.880
There was a man
named Robert Smalls.

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:44.940
He was born in Sumter,
South Carolina.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:45.970
He was born a slave.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.720
He was working on a supply ship.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:52.050
And when his master went to
have dinner on a Friday night,

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:54.660
he stole away with six
of his crew members,

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:59.190
picked up the families, and put
on his master's hat and coat

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:02.730
and went past five
Southern garrisons

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:06.870
and turned the ship
over to the Union Navy.

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:09.840
He was celebrated
throughout the North.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:12.510
And as a consequence
of his brave actions,

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:15.720
Lincoln permitted Blacks
to fight in the Civil War.

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:19.230
Well, after the war, he
became a wealthy businessman.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:22.260
And under
Reconstruction, he served

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:25.530
in the House of Representatives
and became wealthy

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:28.350
and went back and
purchased the plantation

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:31.140
on which he was a slave.

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:34.620
And because the slave
master's wife and children

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:37.290
were destitute, and
she was delusional,

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:40.470
he took them in
and cared for them

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:43.590
and permitted her to sleep
in the master bedroom,

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:47.570
because she didn't
realize slavery had ended.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:55.400
Robert Small is an example
of radical grace in action.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:21:02.600
And so this is a history that
is being kept from this country.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:04.760
And there are endless examples.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:06.740
There were 20
Blacks who were born

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:08.120
slaves who died millionaire.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:11.450
One last story, and I
want to talk about how we

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:14.570
apply all this knowledge today.

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:17.420
Betty Mason was born 1818--

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:22.430
illiterate, Mississippi, walked
behind a wagon of her master,

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:26.390
going from Mississippi
to Salt Lake City.

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:28.070
He was a Mormon.

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:29.180
She had three children.

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:30.180
She tended the sheep.

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.870
One of them was by
the slave master.

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:36.320
When she got there-- it took six
months walking behind a wagon.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:37.670
They moved to California.

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:38.870
It was a free state.

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:41.120
She was freed.

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:42.710
She was a midwife.

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:44.810
She made $1.50 a day.

00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:46.820
She saved her
money for 10 years,

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:50.510
purchased land in what is
now downtown Los Angeles

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:52.910
and made a commercial--
and when she died,

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:55.790
she died a millionaire
and ended up

00:21:55.790 --> 00:21:59.030
being a philanthropist
and founder of the AME

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:00.920
Church there.

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:04.460
There are endless examples of
only in this system of ours

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:08.870
could somebody be born a slave
and die millionaires and live

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:10.010
this kind of life.

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.110
And so the question
is, if people

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:14.630
could do what they did when
whites were at their worst,

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:18.090
Blacks were at their best.

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:23.190
But there are people who
want to conceal these truths

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:30.390
and define Black America by
the birth defect of this nation

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:31.830
rather than by its promise.

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:39.110
The conservative movement
has proprietary interests

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:42.620
that is compatible with
this self-determination.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:46.750
The attitude that exists
then exists today.

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:48.590
Larry talked about Leon Watkins.

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:53.010
Leon was a Robert Smalls.

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:56.970
He demonstrates that when
you have self-determination,

00:22:56.970 --> 00:23:00.270
a will to fight, and
belief in your God,

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:03.450
that there's no barrier
that can hold you back.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:05.830
But what we must do,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:10.110
is that we must apply old
values to a new vision--

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.960
that the spirit of
renewal, that we've

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:16.950
got to take the principles that
operate on our market economy

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:19.920
and apply them to
the social economy.

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:23.040
In our market economy,
only 3% of the people

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:27.940
are entrepreneurs, but they
generate 70% of the jobs.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:30.865
Entrepreneurs tend to be C
students, not A students.

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:38.760
Those A students come back
to universities like this,

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:39.960
and they teach.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.045
C students come back and endow.

00:23:42.045 --> 00:23:43.905
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:49.080
Because smart people
have to have all the

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:50.430
answers before they act.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:54.040
And when they act, the
opportunity is gone.

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:59.300
But C students, like me,
we can fail, get back up,

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:03.070
try again, fail the second
time, and then the third time we

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:04.000
get it.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:07.330
That's the level of
self-determination and grit,

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:09.610
like Leon Watkins did.

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:12.580
And so, ladies and
gentlemen, my message today

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:17.290
is that if we are to retake
this country from those

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:22.120
who would use race
to defame us, we

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:25.900
must not do so by
having white papers

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:30.130
and arguing on Fox against
what the other side is doing.

00:24:30.130 --> 00:24:33.490
We need a ground strategy--

00:24:33.490 --> 00:24:40.690
a ground strategy like
George Whitfield, who was--

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:46.380
went to Pembroke,
1732, in England.

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:48.030
His father was an innkeeper.

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:48.900
He died.

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:54.210
And so George had to clean
the inn where they lived.

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:56.410
He didn't have enough
money to go to college,

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:58.710
and a friend supported him.

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:01.170
But he had to do sort
of a work, and he

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:03.060
served the Wesley brothers.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.160
And at that time,
students at Oxford

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:09.720
could not speak to students
who were upper class.

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.310
But they established
a friendship.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:15.150
He went on to become
a famous preacher.

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:19.240
And also, when he
came to the colonies,

00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:22.780
he did more to unite the
colonies than almost anybody.

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:26.190
But what George did
was he got on horseback

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:31.030
and rode to every single colony,
spreading the word of God.

00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:35.640
He must have spoken directly
to 70% of all of the people

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.620
in our colonies,
which united us.

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:39.240
Ladies and gentlemen,
those of us

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:42.970
who are servants of God, who
wish to make a difference,

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:44.910
we've got to leave
our hallowed halls

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:47.010
and go out, as
George Whitfield did,

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:49.890
and interact
directly with people

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:51.750
who are suffering a problem.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:56.790
But we also must identify people
who exemplify the virtues.

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:59.940
What the left is doing
now is a parasite

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.730
on the civil rights legacy.

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:06.570
They are talking about
justice for George Floyd

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.730
and hijacking the
civil rights movement.

00:26:08.730 --> 00:26:12.270
And then they go to Portland and
burn the Bibles, burn the flag,

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:17.000
and then talk against
the nuclear family.

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.310
These are the most destructive.

00:26:19.310 --> 00:26:23.100
And so we believe that in
order for us to stand up,

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:28.340
we must de-racialize race
and de-segregate poverty.

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:32.030
But we're not going to
do this by, as I said,

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:34.100
issuing white papers,
or complaining

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.390
about what the left is doing.

00:26:36.390 --> 00:26:40.910
We have to join in
partnership with people who I

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:43.700
call the Josephs of this world.

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:50.270
The Woodson Center, when
we were looking for a model

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:56.720
about how can people with
wealth and people who are poor

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:59.660
come together to
form a coalition

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:03.140
to take back this nation,
I found it in Genesis,

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:06.710
in the story of Joseph.

00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:10.242
Joseph, as you know, was from
a dysfunctional Hebrew family.

00:27:10.242 --> 00:27:12.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:17.620
And he was a little
arrogant, too.

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:20.680
And he told his brothers, I
saw you bowing down to me.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:23.673
But that's not the way to endear
yourself with your siblings.

00:27:23.673 --> 00:27:26.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:27.540
And of course, you
know the story,

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:31.530
how they faked his death,
killed an animal, sold them

00:27:31.530 --> 00:27:34.770
to the Ishmaelites, who
were slave traders in Egypt.

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:38.780
And he's found himself
in a house of Potiphar.

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:41.480
But he never became bitter
and became the best servant

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:43.550
in the house of Potiphar.

00:27:43.550 --> 00:27:45.860
And he was a very
handsome young man.

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:47.930
And Potiphar's
wife lusted for him

00:27:47.930 --> 00:27:49.730
and wanted to have sex with him.

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:52.460
But he refused, because
he had lateral integrity

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:53.780
and horizontal integrity.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:59.320
It would have been a violation
of the trust his master had

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:02.100
and also a sin against his God.

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:04.350
And she sent all
the servants out

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:07.110
and said that he tried to
rape her when he ran out.

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:12.660
And he was put in prison, where
he became the best prisoner.

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:14.310
And he rose in the prison.

00:28:14.310 --> 00:28:17.190
And he met the cup
bearer and the baker--

00:28:17.190 --> 00:28:19.320
interpreted their dreams.

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:21.510
And they said they
would help him,

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:23.070
and they turned
their backs on him.

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:27.270
One day the pharaoh
had a dream that none

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:32.700
of his MBAs or his
PhDs could answer.

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:34.970
And then the cup
bearer said, there

00:28:34.970 --> 00:28:39.260
is a Hebrew in the prisons
that has the ability

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:42.160
to interpret dreams.

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:45.100
So Joseph was cleaned up and
brought before us-- and said,

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:46.730
bow down to the god pharaoh.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:49.790
He refused, because it would
be a sin against his God.

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:52.870
So pharaoh knew
he had integrity.

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:54.880
And when pharaoh said
to him, I understand

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:56.500
you can interpret
dreams, he said, no,

00:28:56.500 --> 00:29:00.410
the last time I did that,
I got in a little trouble.

00:29:00.410 --> 00:29:02.450
But he said, God
interprets dreams.

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:06.108
I am merely the
instrument of God's will.

00:29:06.108 --> 00:29:08.150
And he said, there will
be seven years of plenty,

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:09.950
followed by seven
years of famine.

00:29:09.950 --> 00:29:12.660
Save up 20% and
appoint an overseer.

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:15.755
The first time that
we had flat tax.

00:29:15.755 --> 00:29:18.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:19.940 --> 00:29:21.560
And of course, you
know what happened.

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:24.650
He appointed Joseph.

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:26.900
Think about how
powerful that is.

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:30.500
First of all, the
fact that Joseph

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:35.660
was treated unjustly
and imprisoned but never

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:38.610
became bitter.

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:43.230
And Joseph, as a consequence
of his position of power,

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:48.780
was able to save not only
the brothers who betrayed him

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:51.360
but the Egyptians
who enslaved him.

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:56.110
And so he is a powerful model.

00:29:56.110 --> 00:29:59.920
So we use that as a model
to looking for solutions.

00:29:59.920 --> 00:30:03.130
And that's what I want to
talk about-- what solutions.

00:30:03.130 --> 00:30:05.830
We go into the inner
cities, and we look

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:08.170
for the two types of Josephs.

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:11.395
We're looking for people who are
in poverty, but not of poverty.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:17.830
You say 70% of the people are
raising children or dropping

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:18.760
out of school.

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:22.330
It means 30% are not.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:23.470
We go into there.

00:30:23.470 --> 00:30:27.370
We look for people who are
raised-- the Josephs in Bible.

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:29.638
They're in poverty,
not of poverty.

00:30:29.638 --> 00:30:30.680
They're raising children.

00:30:30.680 --> 00:30:33.460
They're not dropping
out of school, in jail.

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:36.670
No one does research,
Larry, or documents

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:40.390
why they're able to
achieve against the odds.

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:41.680
But we do.

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:44.920
They're the Leon
Watkins of this world.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:47.270
And then there's the
other type of Joseph,

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:50.650
that were prostitutes,
that were gang-bangers,

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:54.010
but through God's grace
they had become redeemed.

00:30:54.010 --> 00:30:56.290
And therefore, they
are powerful witnesses

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:58.570
to others in that
environment that redemption

00:30:58.570 --> 00:31:03.520
is available to you if you
just follow this course.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:06.050
Leon Watkins is an example--

00:31:06.050 --> 00:31:08.140
four children, living in
this gang neighborhood.

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:11.920
He had to put his family in
the bathroom when the gangs--

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:16.030
he reached out to 26 members
of the east side Crips.

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:18.350
And when they pulled
up, he walked up to him

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:19.600
and he said, what do you want?

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.330
He said, I'm going to talk
to you about your life.

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:26.380
After sitting on a trash
can for three hours,

00:31:26.380 --> 00:31:32.590
he had Quake, who's still
around, in Bible study.

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:35.290
Four days later, he
had all 26 members

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:37.510
of the gang in Bible study.

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:40.780
They went from being
predators to protectors

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:43.030
of that community.

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:47.270
And one day, Leon got a
call at 2:00 in the morning.

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:50.440
They said, hey, we found
two people burglarizing

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:53.170
our pharmacy.

00:31:53.170 --> 00:31:54.337
What should we do with them?

00:31:54.337 --> 00:31:56.253
So Leon said, what do
you think you should do?

00:31:56.253 --> 00:31:57.700
And he said, we should do them.

00:31:57.700 --> 00:32:00.220
He says, no, you're
peacekeepers now.

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:01.450
Call the police.

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:03.520
So there's a picture
on the front page

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:07.030
of the LA Times of these
gang members with Leon

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:09.790
turning over these burglars.

00:32:09.790 --> 00:32:13.010
And so they became
a force for good.

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:14.950
There's an example
of what Josephs

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:16.360
can do around this country.

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:18.490
We did the same thing
in Washington, DC.

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:22.000
22 years ago, there was an
area called Benning Terrace.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:24.360
There were 53 murders in
a five-square-block area

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:25.330
in two years.

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:29.200
I recruited the Leon
Watkins, or the Josephs,

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:32.960
that had the trust and
confidence of the people.

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:36.030
And a 12-year-old
boy was killed.

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:38.150
And so I sent them
into that neighborhood,

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:42.110
to bring the warring factions
to my office downtown.

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:43.610
They came in separate buses.

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:45.530
They had bulletproof vests on.

00:32:45.530 --> 00:32:47.300
We gave them a
meal-- because kids

00:32:47.300 --> 00:32:49.100
will fight when they're
drinking together,

00:32:49.100 --> 00:32:52.110
but not when they're
eating together.

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:55.980
And they said, no one ever
asked us to be peaceful.

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:59.790
Well, through a series of
meetings, they shook hands.

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:03.330
And they went back
into that community

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:07.290
that they used to terrorize,
now as ambassadors of peace.

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:12.510
Because once their
character changes,

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:16.350
their characteristic
has an advantage.

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:19.040
And so I say 80% of
my closest friends

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:21.640
have letters in front of
their names, not behind them.

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:29.340
But if we can just unite the
pharaohs and the Josephs,

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:31.620
they are the ones in
these communities--

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:37.590
82% of Black surveys are
against defunding the police.

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:40.260
60% of Black surveys
do not believe

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:44.770
racial discrimination is the
principal barrier to their--

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:48.060
but they are not given a voice.

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:51.240
They are the Josephs out there.

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:55.560
And there are people falsely
claiming to represent them.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:59.820
And as a consequence, their
actions are being perverted.

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:04.200
So what we are doing, we
started something called 1776.

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:06.450
We are going around
the gatekeepers

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:09.780
and giving voice
to the voiceless.

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:11.730
And this is what's
going to unite America.

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:16.960
Low-income Blacks are
America's new patriots,

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:18.880
because they are
the ones suffering

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:22.600
the most from the
vilification of police.

00:34:22.600 --> 00:34:25.780
We brought together a group
called Voices of Black Mothers

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:27.100
United--

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:31.750
2,500 of them, who've lost
children to urban violence.

00:34:31.750 --> 00:34:35.420
And they are the ones who took
out a full-page ad that says,

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:38.650
we are not for
defunding the police.

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:40.480
It's important,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:44.770
for America to move
race, because America

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:47.260
is in a moral and
spiritual free fall, that

00:34:47.260 --> 00:34:55.350
is consuming young people
across race and class.

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:59.580
The leading cause of death of
young people in the inner city

00:34:59.580 --> 00:35:01.050
is homicide.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:02.880
The leading cause of
death in Appalachia,

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:07.080
among lower-income whites,
is prescription drugs.

00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:14.130
And the leading cause of death
in Silicon Valley is suicide.

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:18.270
The suicide rate among
teens in Silicon Valley

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:22.020
is six times the
national average,

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:25.650
where there are
two-parent households

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:31.730
with master's degrees, with
a median income of $180,000.

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:35.360
So it isn't just
family composition.

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:40.670
So what our plan is, to bring
together people across race

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:45.470
and class lines-- the
mothers of Appalachia,

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:47.960
Silicon Valley mothers,
and inner-city mothers--

00:35:47.960 --> 00:35:50.870
to come together
to find out, how

00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:55.970
can we exchange strategies to
promote healing and overcoming?

00:35:55.970 --> 00:35:59.730
And what lessons can we teach
those to prevent children

00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:00.230
from--

00:36:00.230 --> 00:36:04.430
but we won't do that, as long as
we are being separated by race.

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:07.520
And so those are the
kinds of coalitions

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:11.210
that we are seeking to do.

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:15.300
And we need the pharaohs and
the Josephs to come together.

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:17.090
And that's why what we need--

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:19.010
Larry, there needs
to be a university.

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:24.050
As conservatives, we
spend too much time

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:27.090
doing failure studies.

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:29.400
That's why we have the
reputation of not caring

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:31.830
about poor and Black folks.

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:34.230
You can learn nothing
from studying failure

00:36:34.230 --> 00:36:37.770
except how to create failure.

00:36:37.770 --> 00:36:41.130
And so what we are
recommending is

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:44.880
that scholars go into
low-income communities

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:46.560
and report on resilience.

00:36:49.820 --> 00:36:51.590
There's an example.

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:54.390
There's a mother--
for three years,

00:36:54.390 --> 00:36:57.950
she has two daughters sleeping
in the back of her car

00:36:57.950 --> 00:36:59.990
and sleeping in
homeless shelters.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:04.600
The girls were studying by
the light on the cell phone.

00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:07.080
One graduated salutatorian.

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:09.360
The other graduated
valedictorian.

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:11.790
They started Spelman
College as sophomore

00:37:11.790 --> 00:37:15.660
because they took so many
advanced-placement classes.

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:19.200
Why don't we know who they are?

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:23.240
There was an 18-year-old Black
homeless boy who borrowed his

00:37:23.240 --> 00:37:27.620
brother's bike, and for six
hours bicycled across the state

00:37:27.620 --> 00:37:29.480
of Georgia, in August--

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:35.030
only to pitch a tent on the
grounds of a community college

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:37.470
that he attended
the year before.

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:39.830
And two white police officers
came over and asked him

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:41.480
what he was doing.

00:37:41.480 --> 00:37:45.020
And once he found out,
they put him up in a motel.

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:47.720
And they wanted to
get him a job and get

00:37:47.720 --> 00:37:52.160
the owner of a restaurant
to give him some money.

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:53.340
He said, I don't want money.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:54.980
I want a job.

00:37:54.980 --> 00:37:57.650
So he started washing dishes.

00:37:57.650 --> 00:38:00.260
Well, someone posted
his experience.

00:38:00.260 --> 00:38:03.800
They raised $80,000.
in three days.

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:06.680
He held a press conference
to thank everybody.

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:10.550
The president of the college
allowed him to move on campus.

00:38:10.550 --> 00:38:13.610
And now he's using that
money to support himself.

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:17.270
There are endless
examples of grace

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:20.750
in action in our society, where
people are coming together

00:38:20.750 --> 00:38:23.550
to help one another--

00:38:23.550 --> 00:38:26.280
that deep in the
DNA of this country

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:31.890
is that desire to reward
the virtues of our Founders.

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:39.480
When a homeless man in Boston
found $40,000 in a knapsack--

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:41.720
and he turned it in.

00:38:41.720 --> 00:38:42.860
And this was posted--

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:45.560
$96,000 in three days.

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:48.350
There are endless examples,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:38:48.350 --> 00:38:52.310
but we don't have anybody
on our side focusing

00:38:52.310 --> 00:38:57.770
on the promotion of health.

00:38:57.770 --> 00:39:01.760
Again, let me conclude by
saying, the answers exist.

00:39:01.760 --> 00:39:06.440
But we have got to join with
the Josephs of this world.

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:11.300
But what's preventing us from
doing so is classism, elitism.

00:39:14.300 --> 00:39:17.450
There are a lot of people,
both left and right of center,

00:39:17.450 --> 00:39:22.070
who believe that
untutored means unwise--

00:39:22.070 --> 00:39:25.070
and therefore they have
little to contribute.

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:28.370
In our market economy, we don't
care whether Bill Gates ever

00:39:28.370 --> 00:39:30.050
went to college.

00:39:30.050 --> 00:39:32.940
We just care about
what he produces.

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:37.740
In our social economy, if you
got the right credentials,

00:39:37.740 --> 00:39:40.380
you can waste a million
dollars, if it's well-intended,

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:41.880
by well-credentialed people.

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:49.680
And so we need to apply some
of the principles in the market

00:39:49.680 --> 00:39:50.280
economy.

00:39:50.280 --> 00:39:55.740
As I've said, the market
economy, we reward outcomes.

00:39:55.740 --> 00:40:01.060
In our social economy,
we reward credentials.

00:40:01.060 --> 00:40:03.610
There was a man, who was
one of my favorites--

00:40:03.610 --> 00:40:06.340
he died not too long ago--

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:07.360
A.G. Gaston.

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:11.170
He was Alabama's first
Black millionaire.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:13.780
He had a sixth-grade education.

00:40:13.780 --> 00:40:17.590
And A.G. Gaston said, it's
better to say "I is rich"

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:20.040
than "I am poor."

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:22.415
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:27.820
And that is a metaphor
for everything

00:40:27.820 --> 00:40:29.500
that I'm trying to tell you--

00:40:32.060 --> 00:40:37.460
that don't get turned off
if someone speak to you

00:40:37.460 --> 00:40:41.390
and they dangle a
participle or break a verb.

00:40:41.390 --> 00:40:47.030
Listen to them anyway, because
sometimes the Josephs can bring

00:40:47.030 --> 00:40:50.330
us the wisest solutions,
even though it may

00:40:50.330 --> 00:40:53.920
come in an unvarnished package.

00:40:53.920 --> 00:40:56.620
God speaks to people
in different ways.

00:40:56.620 --> 00:40:58.990
But, ladies and
gentlemen, this nation

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:02.770
can only be saved when the
good pharaohs and the Josephs

00:41:02.770 --> 00:41:06.860
come together, and we determine
what government will do.

00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:08.210
It won't come the other way.

00:41:08.210 --> 00:41:12.610
But in order for us to develop
that level of cooperation,

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:19.390
we must lower our pretenses,
shed the limits that we have

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:23.380
and what we believe
in other people.

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:28.300
I just want to close with
a quote from Samuel Adams.

00:41:28.300 --> 00:41:33.050
And he wrote February 12, 1779.

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:36.080
He said, "A general dissolution
of principles and manners

00:41:36.080 --> 00:41:39.230
will more surely overthrow
the liberties of America

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:42.080
than the whole force
of a common enemy.

00:41:42.080 --> 00:41:45.560
While the people are virtuous,
they cannot be subdued.

00:41:45.560 --> 00:41:47.570
But when they lose
their virtue, they

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:49.430
will be ready to
surrender their liberties

00:41:49.430 --> 00:41:53.090
to the first external
or internal invader.

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:56.450
If virtue and knowledge are
diffused among the people,

00:41:56.450 --> 00:41:58.340
they will never be
enslaved, and this

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:00.500
will be their great security.

00:42:00.500 --> 00:42:04.640
Neither the wisest Constitution
nor the wisest laws

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:06.590
will secure the
liberty and happiness

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:13.260
of a people whose manners
are universally corrupt."

00:42:13.260 --> 00:42:17.070
We can't just say, we need
lower taxes, limited government.

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:21.420
If you want limited government,
then support the institutions

00:42:21.420 --> 00:42:24.390
that the people use
that prevents them

00:42:24.390 --> 00:42:25.800
from needing government.

00:42:28.310 --> 00:42:31.490
And expectations
should be followed

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:36.590
with the means of providing the
means to meet the expectation.

00:42:36.590 --> 00:42:39.370
And so we as conservatives
have to understand

00:42:39.370 --> 00:42:43.360
that we must leave
our comfortable places

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:50.050
and go out, as Larry has
done, learn to identify,

00:42:50.050 --> 00:42:58.800
respect, support, and learn from
and serve the least of these.

00:42:58.800 --> 00:43:00.510
God bless you.

00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:03.260
[APPLAUSE]

